data.content:
Now I have the full picture. Here's the answer:

---

**What happened:** C# 14 introduces new built-in `Span<T>` conversions. When the compiler builds the expression tree for `array.Contains(num)`, it now binds to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`. Since expression trees **cannot represent calls involving `Span<T>`/`ReadOnlySpan<T>`**, this throws at runtime (the expression tree interpreter doesn't support span-based methods).

**How to fix:** Force the compiler to resolve to the `Enumerable.Contains` overload by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Cast to IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 3: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

All three approaches disambiguate overload resolution so the compiler picks the LINQ `Enumerable.Contains` method, which is representable in an expression tree.
